INT. TAXIDERMY CLASSROOM - NIGHT

Glass eyes in rows. Fox, owl, a badger mid-snarl. A single desk lamp. Moonlight on frozen fur.

PRUDENCE LOVELACE-AKINS, 30s, instructor, lays a half-stuffed heron on the worktable. Across from her, ISAAC TOMLINSON, 20s, her student, wipes his hands on a towel and keeps glancing at the badger.

ISAAC
Did that move?

PRUDENCE
It's a badger, Isaac. It's dead.

ISAAC
It was facing the door before.

PRUDENCE
You're tired.

She picks up a scalpel. Isaac backs toward the shelves.

PRUDENCE (CONT'D)
Come here. Hold the wing.

He does. His fingers tremble on the feather.

Behind them, the badger turns its head.

Neither sees. The lamp flickers. Prudence cuts a clean line along the heron's belly, and Isaac jerks the wing aside. The fox on the shelf shifts a paw. The owl's neck rotates. Prudence reaches for the thread. Isaac bumps the table. A jar of glass eyes rolls. Every eye in the room is suddenly pointed at the two of them.

ISAAC
Prudence.

PRUDENCE
(not looking up)
Hold still.

ISAAC
Prudence!

She looks up.

The room is full of eyes.

Footsteps in the hall. The door swings wide.

JAMES TALLCHIEF, 50s, the night janitor, mop in hand, steps in. He stops. Takes it in.

JAMES
Ladies, gentlemen. And whoever you are.

The animals all freeze mid-turn.

Silence. James sets the mop down. Walks to the badger. Picks it up by the scruff. Turns it around, facing the wall.

JAMES (CONT'D)
They do that when they're lonely.

PRUDENCE
They do WHAT?

JAMES
Somebody leave a radio on for them. Classical.

He leaves. Isaac exhales for the first time in a minute.

Prudence puts down the scalpel. Reaches for the radio.
